# Reproducing Mixup and Investigating Its Effectiveness Under Increasing Label Noise

## Project Overview

This project reproduces the Mixup training strategy introduced in the paper:

**Mixup: Beyond Empirical Risk Minimization**  
Zhang et al., ICLR 2018.

The project investigates whether Mixup improves generalization on CIFAR-10 and how its effectiveness changes when training labels become increasingly noisy.

## Research Question

Can we reproduce the generalization benefit of Mixup on CIFAR-10, and how does its relative effectiveness change as the amount of training-label noise increases?

## Method

We compare two training strategies:

1. Standard training
2. Mixup training

The experiments use CIFAR-10 and a convolutional neural network.

For the extension, we introduce random label corruption at four levels:

- 0% label noise
- 10% label noise
- 20% label noise
- 30% label noise

The test set remains clean for all experiments.

## Results

| Label Noise | Standard Training | Mixup | Mixup Advantage |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0% | 74.58% | 77.17% | +2.59 pp |
| 10% | 68.61% | 69.60% | +0.99 pp |
| 20% | 71.04% | 71.78% | +0.74 pp |
| 30% | 69.54% | 69.49% | -0.05 pp |

## Main Finding

Mixup improved test accuracy over standard training on clean CIFAR-10 data.

However, the relative advantage of Mixup decreased as training-label noise increased. At 30% label noise, the two methods performed essentially the same in our experiment.

## Repository Contents

- `notebooks/` — experiment notebook
- `figures/` — experimental visualizations
- `results/` — numerical results
- `report/` — final project report

## Paper

Zhang et al., "mixup: Beyond Empirical Risk Minimization", ICLR 2018.
